book from the illusionist darren brown  pushes people  to think differently to help overcome  anxiety how does that work  well let's find out from the man himself  hello to you john it's always great to  see  thanks for joining us on the program  this morning as i said the book  encouraging people to think differently  to overcome anxiety tell us how we do  that  uh well it's there are two big uh ideas  in the book one and it's a rewrite of  or a shorter version of a book called  happy that i wrote a few  a few years ago which is a big book it's  quite a commitment this is a smaller  version  two big thoughts one is a stoic  idea the book is largely about stoicism  which is  to only try and control the things that  you're in control of and this is a 2 000  year old recipe for minimizing  unnecessary disturbance and anxiety the  only things you're in control over your  thoughts and your actions  everything else what's going on in the  world what other people do what they  think of you and so on  these are things that are actually  outside of our control and it's  ridiculous to try and control them and  that's how you  you know largely make yourself  frustrated um so you kind of roll with  those things all those things on the  outside you decide just to kind of  uh roll with them and you bring your  center of gravity  uh inside by by separating those two  things  and that's a good recipe for a kind of  robustness and i think that works and  there's another thought which is sort of  the  opposite of that which is to let a  certain amount of anxiety  sit uh as you say we all kind of have  these moments in life all of us where we  are  anxious or what we feel that we've  failed and what life has the upper hand  um and we tend to feel very alone and  and it's actually bizarrely those times  when we're most connected to other  people because this is  it is the actual weight of life it's  when we're strangely  most connected with others and of course  the lockdown is a very literal example  of that here we are on the one hand  very isolated on the other hand sharing  in this  uh extraordinary thing and i think by  leaning  into that feeling of sharedness that can  really help too  but if you take on too much anxiety and  you don't have an outlet there's only  one way that that's going to go isn't  there so how do how do you  of course have to control your anxiety  but there has to be ways that you can  express it and is that turning to other  people for help  yeah i mean we may all feel that i mean  what plenty of people  feel that you know they can thrive in in  in lockdown and that it's  you know they have a relatively good  experience of it i mean that's that's  sort of  some people are definitely having that  while other people are having a terrible  time but i think irrespective of how  much  we think we can thrive on our own we  desperately do need  a connection with other people so as  much as i love the stoic idea of  of this very robust sense of self i  think at the same time as where we can  lean out lean out into a shared uh  experience and turn  sort of sometimes just to turn a feeling  of sadness into a sort of  melancholy uh understanding that this is  these things are  a part of life a part of the human  experience uh  that's important to suggesting  constantly that that importance of  of connection and reaching out and  finding shared experiences is  hugely important quite often in job  interviews they say where do you see  yourself in five years time  um you say be careful of long-term goals  yes i think goals are very good in the  short term uh but  really what i what i suppose this whole  book of my thinking is about is the  opposite  of that kind of goal setting believe in  yourself  super optimistic kind of american model  which bizarrely is based on calvinism it  inherited a protestant  work ethic uh where you can seemingly  make the universe provide just by  believing in yourself enough and the  problem is with that  is that it just doesn't work and then  when life has the upper hand and when  things go badly  you've got nothing to do other than just  to blame yourself and to add your own  add a feeling of failure to your list of  problems the reality is we have all our  aims and our goals on one side of the  graph  the other axis is stuff that life throws  back at you what they used to call  fortune  and we don't have much of a respect for  fortune anymore and we live an  x equals y diagonal line we kind of  meander along that line  uh and sometimes life has the upper hand  and sometimes we do so the key is i  think  not to believe that we can crank that  line up into line with our  aims and goals but to make our peace  with how fate and fortune  uh rolls out from day to day and to roll  with that to make up to live in an  easier accordance with fate  as the stoic said two thousand years ago  yeah that's easier to do darren as  one gets older from my own experience  but not so much when you're younger  no when you're younger it's uh there's a  thing i'm about to be 50 next year  extraordinarily and  there is a thing i think that happens  particularly as you get older that you  you look for the thing that is bigger  than you to serve that thing i think  that's an important part of the kind of  passage into the second half of life  you find meaning by throwing yourself  into something bigger than you  losing yourself in that thing that's not  really the project for the first half of  life but it is for the second  ambition and so on is it sits  uncomfortably with the second half of  life  so yeah there are there are definitely  changes in life i'm also naturally quite  introverted so i sit  comfortably with these things so you  know our general constitutional makeup  makes a big difference as well  but nonetheless i think these are  important ideas that just as part of the  human experience are worth bearing in in  mind  50 my goodness i'm 35 this time um an  illusionist  um how do you um how do you perform on  stage  if you are a natural introvert because a  lot of people  listening to you this morning would  think he can't be an introvert we've  seen him on the telly box  i know and there i am you know saying we  shouldn't try and control things outside  of our control and my job  is to you know control things that i  shouldn't have any control over  um i think that's just part of the  natural  thing that performers have i think it's  quite it's quite common to be quite  i'm not terribly shy but i definitely  have that  that sort of introverted thing and it's  very nice to go out and be this sort of  well-rehearsed charismatic version of  yourself  uh of an evening very happy with that  nice  it's good how are you coping with  lockdown you okay  it's good i'm painting i'm uh i've been  writing the book  and then uh i've been painting i paint  large  portraits which i'm now for the first  time actually uh  selling so i feel like a proper artist i  thought you made a tin of dulux and  doing the bathroom walls not that at all  no no no no paul have you got any  trades is there any there that we can  have a look at no maybe next time maybe  we can chat to you again next week and  you show us some of your work if you  don't mind  that would be lovely that would be a  real treat that's fantastic we'll do  that i'll make sure that somebody gives  you a call it's good to talk to you  thank you  thank you for having me on nice to speak  to you thank you  um it's great wasn't it let's see if we  can get him back on the table

